## std.argv — command-line argument access.
##
## From docs/spec.md §6.4 (System Surfaces): argv is exposed as a
## free-fn form after the user writes `pub import std.argv.{get}`.
##
## v0.1 Tier-1 migration: REAL impl — with one architectural caveat.
## The reference body below is `return __zag_argv;` (the module-level
## global captured by genFun's `is_main` special case via
## `init.minimal.args.toSlice(allocator)` at main entry, because zig
## 0.16 retired `std.os.argv` / `std.posix.argv` entirely).
##
## The body lives here for documentation + scaffold parsing, but the
## actual binding is PREAMBLE-side: `stdlibPreambleName("get")`
## returns `__zag_argv` (src/codegen/core.zig), so the imports loop's
## fast path emits `const get = __zag_argv;` IN THE USER MODULE —
## the @import + per-selector-alias slow path would resolve `get` to
## this file's materialized std/argv.zig, a separate zig module whose
## OWN self-contained preamble copy of `__zag_argv` is never assigned
## (only the user module's global gets captured at main entry).
## Mirrors the String/Writer hardcoded-coupling rationale; the
## pre-migration `argv_get` router row emitted the same `__zag_argv`
## reference inline and is retired.
##
## The slice is bounded by the actual argc (toSlice returns exactly
## argc entries), so no 32-slot cap or per-call blk walker is needed.

pub fun get() -> []const []const u8 {
    return __zag_argv;
}
